"A pigment gives paint its color.": "949b5af5c2a0",
"Each pixel on the screen has a red, a green, and a blue part.": "a6300bde4bb7",
"In art class you may have learned a different set of primary colors: red, yellow, and blue. That set is older than modern color science, and it is still used in painting and in art lessons today. Printers use cyan, magenta, and yellow. Both sets are in use, and neither can do everything, because no three real paints, inks, or lights can mix every color there is. One more thing: the paint on this page is drawn by a screen, which means it is drawn with light. Try the same mixes with real paint and see how close the model comes.": "e691f118a8e5",
"Look again and you will see that the two sets fit together. Where two lamps overlap you get yellow, cyan, and magenta, and those are the three colors of paint in the model. Where two of those paints overlap you get red, green, and blue. A color printer uses cyan, magenta, and yellow ink for this reason. Real inks are not perfect, and the three together usually give a dark gray, so printers add black ink as well. A screen works the other way. Each pixel is usually made of three tiny lamps: one red, one green, and one blue.": "cb3090dc092d",
"Mixing light is additive: each lamp adds more light.": "b730508ee246",
"Mixing paint is subtractive: each layer takes some light away.": "25f2fd32fd07",
"On a white wall, why does the place where a red spot and a green spot overlap look brighter than the red spot alone?": "46c86cafe4f5",
"Paint does not make light. Paint gets its color from a pigment, and a pigment absorbs some of the light that falls on it and reflects the rest. White paper reflects almost all of the light. A layer of yellow paint absorbs most of the blue light. Magenta paint absorbs mostly green light, and cyan paint absorbs mostly red light. Lay yellow over magenta and both the blue and the green are taken away, so red is what is left. Each new layer takes away more light and the mix gets darker. This is called subtractive mixing.": "5765437946f1",
"Red, green, and blue are the primary colors of light.": "4033758b2beb",
"Squeeze yellow, magenta, and cyan paint onto a plate and stir them together. The mix turns into a dark gray that is close to black. Now go into a dark room with three lamps, one red, one green, and one blue, and point all of them at the same place on a white wall. That place turns white. Three colors of paint make something close to black, and three colors of light make white. Why are the two results opposite?": "72b9ae49bed5",
"Squeeze yellow, magenta, and cyan paint onto a plate and stir them together. The mix turns into a dark gray that is close to black. Now go into a dark room with three lamps, one red, one green, and one blue, and point all of them at the same place on a white wall. That place turns white. Three colors of paint make something close to black, and three colors of light make white. Why are the two results opposite? Start with the lamps. A wall in a dark room sends no light to your eyes. Switch on the red lamp, and the wall reflects red light back to you. Switch on the green lamp as well, and the place where the two spots overlap now reflects red light and green light together. More light reaches your eyes, so that place looks brighter, and it looks yellow. This is called additive mixing, because every lamp adds light. Red, green, and blue are called the primary colors of light. Red and green make yellow, green and blue make cyan, and red and blue make magenta. All three together make white. Why yellow? The answer is in your eyes. At the back of each eye are cells called cones, and there are three kinds. Each kind responds to a wide range of light, but one is most sensitive to long waves, one to medium waves, and one to short waves. Your brain works out a color from how strongly the three kinds respond. A certain mix of red and green light stimulates the cones exactly as much as yellow light does, so your eyes cannot tell the two apart. Paint does not make light. Paint gets its color from a pigment, and a pigment absorbs some of the light that falls on it and reflects the rest. White paper reflects almost all of the light. A layer of yellow paint absorbs most of the blue light. Magenta paint absorbs mostly green light, and cyan paint absorbs mostly red light. Lay yellow over magenta and both the blue and the green are taken away, so red is what is left. Each new layer takes away more light and the mix gets darker. This is called subtractive mixing. Look again and you wil